UPDATE public.vault_articles SET title = $v$Nervous System: Three States, One Map$v$, subtitle = $v$Safe, mobilised, shut down, and which tool fits which$v$, summary = $v$Your nervous system runs in three broad states: calm and social, mobilised for a fight, or shut down. Naming the state you are in tells you which tool to reach for, and that is the map this shelf is built on.$v$, evidence_tier = $v$promising$v$, protocol = $v${}$v$::jsonb, benefits = ARRAY[$v$A vocabulary for what the body is doing$v$, $v$Every tool on the shelf mapped to a state$v$, $v$An end to the two-state trap of stressed or calm$v$], risks = ARRAY[$v$Polyvagal theory is contested as neuroscience; use the map as shorthand, not as physics$v$, $v$Trauma responses need a trauma-informed therapist, not a map$v$], body_md = $v$**Three states**
The polyvagal model, from the physiologist Stephen Porges, sorts the autonomic nervous system into three states with clear bodily signatures. Safe and social: warm, open, easy breath, eye contact comes naturally. Mobilised: tight, alert, fast breath, narrow vision, the state of a hard deadline or an argument. Shut down: heavy, numb, foggy, checked out, the state of burnout and the flat days after too many hard ones. Most advice acts as if there were only two states, stressed and calm. The third is what makes the map useful, because it needs different tools.

**Why the map matters**
The wrong tool in the wrong state makes things worse. In shutdown, a slow breathing practice can deepen the freeze; the body needs gentle mobilisation first, a walk, a splash of cold, then the calm tools. In mobilisation, a rest practice can leave you wired underneath. Knowing the state comes before choosing the tool, which is why this is lesson 1.

**What this shelf is honest about**
Polyvagal theory's account of evolution and anatomy is contested by other physiologists, and lesson 2 says exactly where. The three-state map is used here as a clinical shorthand that people find accurate about themselves, not as settled neuroscience. The tools that follow stand on their own trials: slow breathing, guided rest, the dive reflex, the morning routine, and four self-hypnosis scripts with an honest note on what hypnosis is.

**What this shelf skips**
Vagus nerve anatomy at length. Devices that promise to stimulate it, most of which have no outcome data. Trauma protocols, which belong with a trauma-informed therapist.

**How to read this shelf**
Eleven lessons in order. Name the state three times a day this week, and the rest of the shelf will make sense faster.$v$, why_it_matters = $v$You cannot regulate what you cannot name. Most people move between mobilised and shut down all day and call both of them stress. The map turns that into a read you can act on.$v$, try_today = ARRAY[$v$Set three alarms today. At each, name the state: safe, mobilised or shut down.$v$, $v$Note what put you there.$v$, $v$Note what brought you back, a person, a place, a walk; that is your first tool.$v$], key_takeaways = ARRAY[$v$Three states: safe and social, mobilised, shut down.$v$, $v$Name the state before you pick the tool; the wrong tool deepens the wrong state.$v$, $v$The map is a clinical shorthand; the tools have their own evidence.$v$], quiz = $v$[{"q": "How many states does the map describe?", "choices": ["Three: safe and social, mobilised, shut down", "Five", "Two, stressed and calm"], "correct": 0, "explain": "The third state, shutdown, is what makes the map useful: burnout and numbness live there and need different tools."}, {"q": "Why can a calming tool make things worse?", "choices": ["It cannot", "In shutdown, calming deepens the freeze; the body needs gentle mobilisation first", "Tools always work"], "correct": 1, "explain": "Tools push in a direction. Knowing the state tells you which direction to push."}]$v$::jsonb, references_json = $v$[{"author": "Porges, S. W.", "title": "The Polyvagal Theory: Neurophysiological Foundations of Emotions, Attachment, Communication, and Self-Regulation", "year": 2011}, {"author": "Dana, D.", "title": "The Polyvagal Theory in Therapy: Engaging the Rhythm of Regulation", "year": 2018}, {"author": "Grossman, P.", "title": "Fundamental challenges and likely refutations of the five basic premises of the polyvagal theory (Biol. Psychol.)", "year": 2023}]$v$::jsonb, reflect_prompt = $v$Which of the three states do you spend most of a working day in, honestly?$v$, integrate_prompt = $v$A week of naming the state. Which tool did you reach for, and did it match?$v$, practice_minutes = 3, lesson_number = 1, display_order = 1, course_role = $v$foundations$v$, read_time_min = 2, updated_at = now() WHERE slug = 'foundations-nervous-system';

UPDATE public.vault_articles SET title = $v$Polyvagal Theory: What It Claims and What Holds$v$, subtitle = $v$The theory, its critics, and the practices that survive either way$v$, summary = $v$Polyvagal theory says the vagus nerve runs on cues of safety, not commands, which is why being told to calm down never works. The theory's anatomy is disputed; the practices it points to have their own evidence, and this lesson keeps those.$v$, evidence_tier = $v$speculative$v$, protocol = $v${"duration": "5 min", "frequency": "Daily for four weeks; on demand by state", "intensity": "Four in, eight out · humming · a soft gaze", "prerequisites": "None; trauma history with a trauma-informed practitioner"}$v$::jsonb, benefits = ARRAY[$v$A list of inputs the body actually responds to$v$, $v$Tools matched to the state instead of generic calm-down advice$v$, $v$Compatible with therapy$v$], risks = ARRAY[$v$The neuroscience is disputed; the chip says so$v$, $v$Trauma: with a trauma-informed practitioner$v$, $v$Not a substitute for clinical care$v$], body_md = $v$**The claim**
Porges (2011) proposed that the vagus nerve has two branches of different evolutionary ages, that the newer branch supports social engagement and the older one shutdown, and that the nervous system reads safety from faces, voices and breath before the thinking mind gets a vote. The practical translation: you cannot order yourself calm. You can give the body the inputs it reads as safe: a long exhale, a warm voice, a steady face, slow movement.

**The critics**
Grossman (2023) went through the theory's five founding premises and argues that the anatomy and the evolutionary story do not hold up: the branch distinction is not what the theory says it is, and the measures used for vagal tone do not track what they are claimed to track. Other physiologists agree. That is why this lesson wears the speculative chip: the framework is a story that many clinicians find useful, and it is not established physiology.

**What survives**
The practices do not depend on the story. Slow breathing with a long exhale raises heart-rate variability and lowers arousal across a meta-analysis of 223 studies (Laborde et al. 2022). Social contact and a calm voice lower stress physiology in ways measured for decades. Movement discharges mobilisation. These work whether or not the vagus nerve has the branches Porges described.

**The toolkit by state**
Mobilised, racing, angry: a long exhale, four counts in and eight out, for two minutes; cold water on the face, which is lesson 5; a walk or a push against a wall to spend the charge. Shut down, numb, flat: do not try to relax further; move gently first, a slow walk, a cold splash, then the calm tools. To stay in the safe state: people you trust, a face to look at, humming or singing, easy movement, time outside.

**A daily five minutes**
Two minutes of four in, eight out. One minute of humming on the exhale. Two minutes of a soft gaze at something or someone you like. Daily for four weeks, and the route back becomes familiar.$v$, why_it_matters = $v$Telling a mobilised body to relax is like telling a sprinting heart to slow on command. The useful part of the theory is the list of inputs the body actually responds to; use those and leave the story to the researchers.$v$, try_today = ARRAY[$v$Soften the jaw, drop the shoulders, exhale for eight counts. Hold that for one minute.$v$, $v$Look at someone you trust today and notice what your breath does.$v$, $v$Find the one place or person that reliably puts you back in the safe state, and go there this week.$v$], key_takeaways = ARRAY[$v$The body reads safety from breath, faces and voices, not from instructions.$v$, $v$The theory's anatomy is disputed; the practices have their own trials.$v$, $v$Mobilised: exhale and move. Shut down: move first, then calm.$v$], quiz = $v$[{"q": "Why does the chip say speculative?", "choices": ["Nobody has tested breathing", "The practices do not work", "The theory's anatomy and evolutionary story are disputed; the practices stand on separate evidence"], "correct": 2, "explain": "Grossman 2023 and others challenge the premises. The long exhale, the voice and the company have trials of their own."}, {"q": "In shutdown, what comes first?", "choices": ["Gentle mobilisation, then the calm tools", "Caffeine", "Deep relaxation"], "correct": 0, "explain": "Relaxing a frozen body deepens the freeze. Move a little, then settle."}, {"q": "Laborde's 2022 meta-analysis found that slow breathing…", "choices": ["Does nothing", "Raises heart-rate variability and lowers arousal", "Only works with a device"], "correct": 1, "explain": "223 studies. The long exhale is the part that does the work, device or no device."}]$v$::jsonb, references_json = $v$[{"author": "Porges, S. W.", "title": "The Polyvagal Theory: Neurophysiological Foundations of Emotions, Attachment, Communication, and Self-Regulation", "year": 2011}, {"author": "Grossman, P.", "title": "Fundamental challenges and likely refutations of the five basic premises of the polyvagal theory (Biol. Psychol.)", "year": 2023}, {"author": "Laborde, S. et al.", "title": "Effects of voluntary slow breathing on heart rate and heart rate variability: a systematic review and a meta-analysis (Neurosci. Biobehav. Rev.)", "year": 2022}, {"author": "Dana, D.", "title": "The Polyvagal Theory in Therapy: Engaging the Rhythm of Regulation", "year": 2018}]$v$::jsonb, reflect_prompt = $v$When someone tells you to calm down, what happens in your body, and what would have worked instead?$v$, integrate_prompt = $v$Four weeks of the daily five minutes. Is the route back to the safe state shorter?$v$, practice_minutes = 5, lesson_number = 2, display_order = 2, course_role = $v$protocol$v$, read_time_min = 3, updated_at = now() WHERE slug = 'polyvagal-fundamentals';

UPDATE public.vault_articles SET title = $v$NSDR and Yoga Nidra$v$, subtitle = $v$Guided rest for a short night, and what one small scan showed$v$, summary = $v$Non-sleep deep rest is a guided body scan that takes you to the edge of sleep without crossing it. Ten to twenty minutes restores attention after a short night; the dopamine claim rests on one scan of eight people, so treat it as promising.$v$, evidence_tier = $v$promising$v$, protocol = $v${"duration": "10 to 20 min", "frequency": "After a short night; three or four times a week if it helps", "intensity": "Lying down, eyes closed, a guided voice", "prerequisites": "A quiet room and a recording"}$v$::jsonb, benefits = ARRAY[$v$Attention back after a short night$v$, $v$Lower stress and better sleep in a month-long trial$v$, $v$No skill required$v$], risks = ARRAY[$v$Some people fall asleep; set an alarm if it matters$v$, $v$Lying flat hurts some backs; bend the knees$v$, $v$Trauma-sensitive: choose a teacher with the training$v$], body_md = $v$**What it is**
Yoga nidra is a guided practice from the yoga tradition: lie down, follow a voice through the body, breathe slowly, rest in a state between waking and sleep. Non-sleep deep rest, a name from the neuroscientist Andrew Huberman, is the same practice without the tradition. A session runs ten to twenty minutes: lie flat, eyes closed; attention moves slowly from the feet to the head, releasing each region; long, slow nasal breathing; a period of simple rest; then small movements and sitting up.

**What the evidence says**
Kjaer et al. (2002) scanned eight experienced practitioners during yoga nidra and measured a 65% rise in dopamine release in the striatum. It is the most quoted number in this field and it comes from eight people in one study: a promising signal, not a finding to build on. Sturdier: Datta et al. (2021) ran a randomised trial in chronic insomnia and found yoga nidra improved sleep quality and daytime function; Moszeik et al. (2022) gave an eleven-minute recording to more than 300 people for a month and measured less stress and better sleep and wellbeing against a waiting list. The state itself sits in the slow, theta-heavy range seen at sleep onset, which is where attention and motor learning seem to consolidate.

**When to use it**
After a short night, twenty minutes around midday is the closest thing to a repayment there is. In the afternoon dip, instead of a coffee after the cut-off. Before creative work, to clear the desk in the head. Not late at night if it wakes you rather than settles you; some people find it activating.

**How to start**
A free guided recording, ten minutes, lying flat or reclined with the knees bent if the back complains. Speakers are fine. Falling asleep in the first sessions is fine too; set an alarm if you must be somewhere. Build to twenty minutes if it serves you.

**What it is not**
Sleep. It is a deposit against a bad night, and the Recovery shelf is where the night itself gets fixed.$v$, why_it_matters = $v$You will not always sleep enough. Twenty minutes of guided rest is the most useful thing to do about a short night, it is free, and it needs no skill.$v$, try_today = ARRAY[$v$Find a ten-minute guided recording and lie down with it today, ideally in the early afternoon.$v$, $v$Knees bent if the back complains; an alarm if you have somewhere to be.$v$, $v$Note your attention in the hour after, against a normal afternoon.$v$], key_takeaways = ARRAY[$v$Ten to twenty minutes of guided rest at the edge of sleep.$v$, $v$The dopamine number comes from eight people; the sleep trials are the sturdier evidence.$v$, $v$A deposit against a short night, not a substitute for the night.$v$], quiz = $v$[{"q": "Where does the 65% dopamine figure come from?", "choices": ["A survey", "A large trial", "One scan of eight experienced practitioners"], "correct": 2, "explain": "Kjaer 2002. Real, small, and quoted far beyond its weight."}, {"q": "When is it most useful?", "choices": ["After a short night or in the afternoon dip", "Right before a hard session", "When already well rested"], "correct": 0, "explain": "It repays a debt. With no debt there is little to repay."}]$v$::jsonb, references_json = $v$[{"author": "Kjaer, T. W. et al.", "title": "Increased dopamine tone during meditation-induced change of consciousness (Cogn. Brain Res.)", "year": 2002}, {"author": "Datta, K., Tripathi, M., Verma, M., Masiwal, D. & Mallick, H. N.", "title": "Yoga nidra practice shows improvement in sleep in patients with chronic insomnia: a randomized controlled trial (Natl. Med. J. India)", "year": 2021}, {"author": "Moszeik, E. N., von Oertzen, T. & Renner, K.-H.", "title": "Effectiveness of a short yoga nidra meditation on stress, sleep, and well-being in a large and diverse sample (Curr. Psychol.)", "year": 2022}]$v$::jsonb, reflect_prompt = $v$What do you usually do with a bad night: push through, caffeinate, or rest?$v$, integrate_prompt = $v$After a week with one session on the short days, did the afternoons change?$v$, practice_minutes = 15, lesson_number = 3, display_order = 3, course_role = $v$protocol$v$, read_time_min = 3, updated_at = now() WHERE slug = 'nsdr-yoga-nidra';

UPDATE public.vault_articles SET title = $v$Coherent Breathing$v$, subtitle = $v$About six breaths a minute, no holds, five minutes to start$v$, summary = $v$Breathing at about six breaths a minute, five seconds in and five out, is the rhythm where heart-rate variability peaks. Heart rate and blood pressure swing together at that rate. Five minutes a day, built to ten or twenty, trains the baseline.$v$, evidence_tier = $v$promising$v$, protocol = $v${"duration": "5 min, building to 10 or 20", "frequency": "Daily, the same slot", "intensity": "About six breaths a minute, 5 to 6 s in and out, no holds", "prerequisites": "None"}$v$::jsonb, benefits = ARRAY[$v$Higher heart-rate variability during the practice and, over weeks, at rest$v$, $v$Lower blood pressure within the session in hypertension$v$, $v$Large reductions in reported stress and anxiety in the pooled biofeedback trials$v$], risks = ARRAY[$v$Forcing the breath brings light-headedness; keep it gentle and quiet$v$, $v$Asthma: keep the exhale unforced$v$, $v$There are no holds in this pattern; if a recording adds them, it is a different practice$v$], body_md = $v$**The resonance frequency**
Heart rate rises on the inhale and falls on the exhale. Breathe at around six breaths a minute and those swings line up with the blood-pressure reflex, so the variability of the heart rate reaches its maximum for that person; Lehrer and Gevirtz (2014) lay out the mechanism and why biofeedback clinics train exactly this rate. The precise number varies from about 4.5 to 7 breaths a minute between people, and 5.5 to 6 is where most land.

**The pattern**
In through the nose for five to six seconds, out through the nose for five to six seconds. No holds; the smooth turn at the top and the bottom is the point. Quiet enough that nobody could hear it; if they could, slow down. Sit upright, shoulders soft. Five minutes a day in week one, ten by week four, up to twenty if you want the full effect.

**What it does during the practice**
Heart-rate variability rises sharply while you breathe this way, and the baroreflex, the system that steadies blood pressure, engages. Joseph et al. (2005) showed that slow breathing at six a minute improved baroreflex sensitivity and lowered blood pressure within the session in people with hypertension.

**What it does over weeks**
Goessl et al. (2017) pooled 24 studies of heart-rate variability biofeedback, which is this breathing with a screen, and found a large reduction in self-reported stress and anxiety. Laborde et al. (2022) pooled the slow-breathing studies more broadly and found higher variability and lower arousal. Resting variability rises over four to eight weeks of daily practice in the biofeedback trials; that is the baseline shift the HRV lesson on the Recovery shelf can show you.

**How to keep the rhythm**
A metronome or any paced-breathing timer at six a minute; the brand is irrelevant. If six seconds feels too slow, start at four and add half a second each week. Morning before work, the afternoon dip, or before bed all work; the same slot each day works best.$v$, why_it_matters = $v$Variability of the heart rate is the cleanest measure of a nervous system that can recover, and this is the most direct way to train it: no device needed, five minutes, most days.$v$, try_today = ARRAY[$v$Set a five-minute timer. In for five seconds, out for five, through the nose, no holds.$v$, $v$Use a paced-breathing timer if counting spoils it.$v$, $v$Same slot tomorrow; the adaptation is in the repetition.$v$], key_takeaways = ARRAY[$v$About six breaths a minute, five to six seconds each way, no holds.$v$, $v$Variability peaks at this rate and the blood-pressure reflex engages.$v$, $v$Five minutes a day, built to twenty; the baseline shifts over four to eight weeks.$v$], quiz = $v$[{"q": "What breathing rate is the target?", "choices": ["Twelve a minute", "About six a minute", "Twenty a minute"], "correct": 1, "explain": "Roughly ten seconds per breath, where the heart-rate swings and the blood-pressure reflex line up."}, {"q": "What did Goessl's 2017 pooled analysis find for this training?", "choices": ["Higher blood pressure", "No effect", "A large reduction in self-reported stress and anxiety"], "correct": 2, "explain": "24 studies of heart-rate variability biofeedback, which is this breathing with a screen."}, {"q": "How long until the resting baseline shifts?", "choices": ["Four to eight weeks of daily practice", "Years", "One session"], "correct": 0, "explain": "The session effect is immediate; the baseline follows the repetition."}]$v$::jsonb, references_json = $v$[{"author": "Lehrer, P. M. & Gevirtz, R.", "title": "Heart rate variability biofeedback: how and why does it work? (Front. Psychol.)", "year": 2014}, {"author": "Joseph, C. N. et al.", "title": "Slow breathing improves arterial baroreflex sensitivity and decreases blood pressure in essential hypertension (Hypertension)", "year": 2005}, {"author": "Goessl, V. C., Curtiss, J. E. & Hofmann, S. G.", "title": "The effect of heart rate variability biofeedback training on stress and anxiety: a meta-analysis (Psychol. Med.)", "year": 2017}, {"author": "Laborde, S. et al.", "title": "Effects of voluntary slow breathing on heart rate and heart rate variability: a systematic review and a meta-analysis (Neurosci. Biobehav. Rev.)", "year": 2022}]$v$::jsonb, reflect_prompt = $v$How does your breathing sound right now, and when did you last hear it slow down?$v$, integrate_prompt = $v$Four weeks in. Did the seven-day HRV trend move, and did anything else move with it?$v$, practice_minutes = 5, lesson_number = 4, display_order = 4, course_role = $v$protocol$v$, read_time_min = 3, updated_at = now() WHERE slug = 'coherent-breathing-5-5';

UPDATE public.vault_articles SET title = $v$Cold Face: The Dive Reflex$v$, subtitle = $v$Thirty seconds of cold water on the face to break a spike$v$, summary = $v$Cold water on the face with a held breath fires the mammalian dive reflex, and the heart slows within seconds. Clinicians use it to break panic; it is the fastest override there is, for acute use only.$v$, evidence_tier = $v$promising$v$, protocol = $v${"duration": "15 to 60 s", "frequency": "On demand, acute use only", "intensity": "About 10 °C water on the face with a breath hold, or an ice pack across the forehead and cheeks", "prerequisites": "A healthy heart; arrhythmia or heart disease means a clinician first"}$v$::jsonb, benefits = ARRAY[$v$Heart rate down within seconds$v$, $v$A crisis skill used in clinical practice$v$, $v$Free, anywhere with a sink$v$], risks = ARRAY[$v$Known arrhythmia or heart disease: not without medical clearance$v$, $v$Cold sensitivity disorders: shorten it$v$, $v$Already shivering cold: the opposite of what you need$v$], body_md = $v$**The reflex**
Every air-breathing mammal has it. Cold on the face, especially the forehead and around the eyes, combined with a held breath, triggers a drop in heart rate and a narrowing of blood vessels in the limbs, so oxygen is kept for the brain and heart. Foster and Sheel (2005) review the human version: the heart rate falls by a tenth to a quarter within seconds, and the effect is stronger the colder the water and the longer the hold. Schipke and Pelzer (2001) showed that face immersion with a breath hold does more than either alone. It evolved for diving. It doubles as the most reliable acute brake on a mobilised nervous system.

**How to use it**
Fill a basin with cold water, about 10 °C, ice if needed. Hold the breath, bend forward and put the face in from forehead to chin for 15 to 30 seconds. Repeat once or twice. Without a basin: a flexible ice pack or a cold wet cloth pressed firmly across the forehead, eyes and cheeks, breath held, 30 to 60 seconds.

**Where it is used**
Marsha Linehan's dialectical behaviour therapy teaches it first among the crisis skills, the temperature step in the sequence temperature, intense exercise, paced breathing (Linehan 2014), for panic, rage and dissociation, when nothing slower would land. Emergency physicians use facial immersion as one of the vagal manoeuvres for a racing heart rhythm before reaching for a drug. The clinical use is established practice; there is no trial of it for panic as such, which is why the chip says promising and not strong.

**When to reach for it**
The start of a panic attack. A rage that is about to say something. Dissociation, when you need to come back into the body. Anxious arousal that will not let you sleep: once, then lie down.

**What it is not**
A cold plunge; different mechanism, different goal, and the Recovery shelf owns that dose. A daily practice; the contrast is the medicine, and it fades if used all the time. Safe for everyone: a known arrhythmia or heart disease means a clinician first, because the reflex is a real cardiac event.$v$, why_it_matters = $v$In a spike the body needs an override that does not go through the thinking mind. Cold on the face is hard-wired, works in seconds, and costs a basin of water.$v$, try_today = ARRAY[$v$Fill a bowl with cold water, ice if you have it.$v$, $v$Hold the breath, face in for 15 to 30 seconds, or a cold pack pressed across the forehead and cheeks.$v$, $v$Repeat once. Notice the heart and the head afterwards.$v$], key_takeaways = ARRAY[$v$Cold, face, breath hold: the dive reflex slows the heart in seconds.$v$, $v$For panic, rage and dissociation; not for chronic stress, and not daily.$v$, $v$A clinical crisis skill, not a trial result; arrhythmia means a clinician first.$v$], quiz = $v$[{"q": "Why does cold on the face slow the heart so fast?", "choices": ["Distraction", "The dive reflex, triggered by cold receptors on the face during a breath hold", "Adrenaline"], "correct": 1, "explain": "Foster and Sheel 2005: a tenth to a quarter off the heart rate within seconds, stronger with colder water and a longer hold."}, {"q": "What is it best for?", "choices": ["Building muscle", "Daily calm", "An acute spike: panic, rage, dissociation"], "correct": 2, "explain": "It is a brake, and a brake used all day stops working. Keep it for the spike."}]$v$::jsonb, references_json = $v$[{"author": "Foster, G. E. & Sheel, A. W.", "title": "The human diving response, its function, and its control (Scand. J. Med. Sci. Sports)", "year": 2005}, {"author": "Schipke, J. D. & Pelzer, M.", "title": "Effect of immersion, submersion, and scuba diving on heart rate variability (Br. J. Sports Med.)", "year": 2001}, {"author": "Linehan, M. M.", "title": "DBT Skills Training Manual, 2nd ed.", "year": 2014}]$v$::jsonb, reflect_prompt = $v$What is your fastest spike, and what do you usually do in its first thirty seconds?$v$, integrate_prompt = $v$You used cold on the face once in a real spike. What happened in the minute after?$v$, practice_minutes = 3, lesson_number = 5, display_order = 5, course_role = $v$protocol$v$, read_time_min = 3, updated_at = now() WHERE slug = 'cold-face-dive-reflex';

UPDATE public.vault_articles SET title = $v$Vagal Tone: Five Daily Builders$v$, subtitle = $v$The unglamorous things that raise the brake over weeks$v$, summary = $v$Vagal tone is how quickly the body's brake brings you back after stress, read as heart-rate variability. It rises over weeks with five ordinary inputs: slow breathing, singing or humming, cold on the face, exercise and sleep, and time with people you trust.$v$, evidence_tier = $v$promising$v$, protocol = $v${"duration": "A few minutes", "frequency": "Most days, two or three of the five", "intensity": "Slow breathing · voice · cold on the face · training and sleep · people", "prerequisites": "None"}$v$::jsonb, benefits = ARRAY[$v$Faster recovery after stress$v$, $v$Better emotional regulation$v$, $v$A rising HRV trend over weeks$v$], risks = ARRAY[$v$These are supports, not treatments for an anxiety disorder$v$, $v$One bad night erases a week of trend; read the trend, not the day$v$], body_md = $v$**What tone means**
The vagus nerve carries the parasympathetic signal that slows the heart and settles the gut, the brake on the stress response. Tone is how strong and how quick that brake is, and it shows in the variability of the heart rate: a heart under strong vagal influence speeds up and slows down with every breath. Higher tone means faster recovery after a hard moment. It is partly inherited, it falls with age and with poor sleep, and it is trainable.

**Five builders**
Slow breathing. Around six breaths a minute, a few minutes most days; Laborde et al. (2022) pooled the studies and found it the most direct lever there is. Lesson 4 owns the dose.
Voice. Singing entrains the breath and with it the heart: Vickhoff et al. (2013) found choir singers' heart rates rising and falling together with the phrases of the song. Humming on the exhale does a smaller version of the same thing and can be done in a car.
Cold on the face. The dive reflex from lesson 5, used occasionally, exercises the brake.
Exercise and sleep. Sandercock et al. (2005) pooled the training studies: regular aerobic exercise raises resting variability. The Recovery shelf's HRV lesson shows that sleep and alcohol move it more than anything else.
People. Kok et al. (2013) followed adults over nine weeks and found that felt connection to others and vagal tone rose together, each feeding the other. Warm contact is a builder, not a reward for building.

**How to train it**
Pick two or three of the five and do them most days for a month. Read the seven-day HRV average by the Recovery shelf's method, not the daily number. Expect the trend to move over weeks, not days, and expect one bad night to erase a week of it, which is information, not failure.

**What the evidence is not**
There is no trial that trains the five together and reads mortality at the end. Each builder has its own evidence, the shared measure is variability, and the claim here is modest: a little, most days, raises the baseline over weeks.$v$, why_it_matters = $v$Recovery after stress is a capacity, and it is trained the way strength is: a little load, most days, with sleep. The five builders are the load, and most of them cost nothing.$v$, try_today = ARRAY[$v$Three minutes at six breaths a minute today.$v$, $v$Hum or sing on the way somewhere; the car counts.$v$, $v$Book one hour this week with someone who makes your shoulders drop.$v$], key_takeaways = ARRAY[$v$Tone is the strength of the brake, read as heart-rate variability.$v$, $v$Five builders: slow breathing, voice, cold on the face, exercise and sleep, people.$v$, $v$Two or three of them most days, judged by the seven-day trend over a month.$v$], quiz = $v$[{"q": "What does higher vagal tone mean in practice?", "choices": ["Faster recovery after a hard moment", "Lower fitness", "A slower brain"], "correct": 0, "explain": "The brake is stronger and quicker. That shows as more variability in the heart rate."}, {"q": "Which builder did Kok's 2013 study track alongside felt connection?", "choices": ["Caffeine", "Vagal tone, rising with connection over nine weeks", "Supplements"], "correct": 1, "explain": "Connection and tone rose together, each feeding the other. Company is a builder."}]$v$::jsonb, references_json = $v$[{"author": "Laborde, S. et al.", "title": "Effects of voluntary slow breathing on heart rate and heart rate variability: a systematic review and a meta-analysis (Neurosci. Biobehav. Rev.)", "year": 2022}, {"author": "Vickhoff, B. et al.", "title": "Music structure determines heart rate variability of singers (Front. Psychol.)", "year": 2013}, {"author": "Sandercock, G. R. H., Bromley, P. D. & Brodie, D. A.", "title": "Effects of exercise on heart rate variability: inferences from meta-analysis (Med. Sci. Sports Exerc.)", "year": 2005}, {"author": "Kok, B. E. et al.", "title": "How positive emotions build physical health: perceived positive social connections account for the upward spiral between positive emotions and vagal tone (Psychol. Sci.)", "year": 2013}]$v$::jsonb, reflect_prompt = $v$After a hard day, how long does it take your body to come back, and what usually brings it?$v$, integrate_prompt = $v$A month of two or three builders. What did the seven-day trend do, and what did the evenings feel like?$v$, practice_minutes = 5, lesson_number = 6, display_order = 6, course_role = $v$protocol$v$, read_time_min = 3, updated_at = now() WHERE slug = 'vagal-tone-builders';

UPDATE public.vault_articles SET title = $v$The First Thirty Minutes$v$, subtitle = $v$Light, water, movement and breath before the phone$v$, summary = $v$The first half hour biases the whole day's nervous system. Light before the phone, a glass of water, five minutes of movement, two minutes of slow breathing: ten focused minutes that make the day proactive instead of reactive.$v$, evidence_tier = $v$promising$v$, protocol = $v${"duration": "10 min", "frequency": "Every morning", "intensity": "Light · water · move · breathe, then the phone", "prerequisites": "None"}$v$::jsonb, benefits = ARRAY[$v$Steadier energy and mood through the day$v$, $v$Better sleep that night, through the clock$v$, $v$A day that starts on your terms$v$], risks = ARRAY[$v$Adapt it to the household; consistency beats completeness$v$, $v$Winter mornings before dawn: a bright lamp, and the Recovery shelf has the numbers$v$], body_md = $v$**Why the first half hour**
The nervous system reads the first inputs of the day as the forecast. A phone in bed delivers other people's demands before you have stood up, and the body starts mobilised. Light, water and movement deliver the opposite: the clock set, the blood volume back, the muscles awake, and only then the inbox.

**The stack**
Light first, phone later. Outside or at a bright window for 5–10 minutes within 60 minutes of waking; Blume et al. (2019) review how morning light sets the clock and lifts mood. The Recovery shelf's lesson on morning light owns the dose and the weather adjustments; this is the same act in its place in the morning.
Water. A glass on waking; the night is eight hours of losses, and the Nutrition shelf's hydration lesson has the rest.
Move. Five minutes of easy movement: a walk to the corner, the mobility routine from the Training shelf, a few squats. Enough to raise the heart rate a little, not a session.
Breathe or sit. Two minutes of slow breathing at six a minute, or the physiological sigh from the Mind shelf, before the first demand arrives.

**The rule**
Ten focused minutes done daily beat an elaborate routine abandoned in week two. The order matters more than the length: light, water, move, breathe, then the phone. If only one survives a bad morning, keep the light.

**What it is not**
A performance. Nobody needs to see it, and the version that fits a school run is the right version. The point is to regulate before you react, and the first thirty minutes are when that costs least.$v$, why_it_matters = $v$You will react to something today. The first thirty minutes decide whether you meet it mobilised or regulated, and the four inputs that decide it are free.$v$, try_today = ARRAY[$v$Tomorrow: light and a glass of water before you touch the phone.$v$, $v$Five minutes of easy movement, then two minutes of slow breathing.$v$, $v$Leave the phone in another room until all four are done.$v$], key_takeaways = ARRAY[$v$Light, water, move, breathe, then the phone.$v$, $v$Ten consistent minutes beat an elaborate routine you drop.$v$, $v$Regulate before you react; the morning is the cheapest place to do it.$v$], quiz = $v$[{"q": "What comes first?", "choices": ["Coffee", "The phone", "Light, outside or at a bright window"], "correct": 2, "explain": "The clock is set by light in the first hour; everything else in the stack works better once it is."}, {"q": "If only one step survives a bad morning?", "choices": ["The light", "The squats", "The breathing"], "correct": 0, "explain": "Light sets tonight's sleep as well as today's mood. It is the one with the longest reach."}]$v$::jsonb, references_json = $v$[{"author": "Blume, C., Garbazza, C. & Spitschan, M.", "title": "Effects of light on human circadian rhythms, sleep and mood (Somnologie)", "year": 2019}, {"author": "Khalsa, S. B. S., Jewett, M. E., Cajochen, C. & Czeisler, C. A.", "title": "A phase response curve to single bright light pulses in human subjects (J. Physiol.)", "year": 2003}]$v$::jsonb, reflect_prompt = $v$What is the first thing your eyes see in the morning, and what does it ask of you?$v$, integrate_prompt = $v$A week of light before the phone. How did the first hour at work compare?$v$, practice_minutes = 10, lesson_number = 7, display_order = 7, course_role = $v$protocol$v$, read_time_min = 2, updated_at = now() WHERE slug = 'morning-protocol-set-the-day';

UPDATE public.vault_articles SET title = $v$Self-Hypnosis: The Sleep Script$v$, subtitle = $v$How these four scripts work, and the one that walks you into sleep$v$, summary = $v$Self-hypnosis is deliberate relaxation, a narrowed focus and a suggestion you choose, and the scripts here are that and nothing more. This one is for sleep onset: a body release, a slow count, and a line that becomes the cue for the night.$v$, evidence_tier = $v$promising$v$, protocol = $v${"duration": "5 to 10 min, in bed", "frequency": "Nightly", "intensity": "Body release, a count from twenty, one line", "prerequisites": "The Recovery shelf's basics in place"}$v$::jsonb, benefits = ARRAY[$v$A repeatable cue for sleep onset$v$, $v$A job for the mind's last ten minutes$v$, $v$More deep sleep in suggestible people, in one study$v$], risks = ARRAY[$v$Not a treatment for insomnia; weeks of poor sleep go to a clinician$v$, $v$If the script wakes you up, shorten the count or drop the imagery$v$], body_md = $v$**How these scripts work**
The four scripts on this shelf share one scaffold: settle the breath, count down into a relaxed and narrowed attention, rehearse or suggest something, and count back up. That is what self-hypnosis is: relaxation plus focused imagination plus a suggestion you have chosen. It is not sleep, not a trance that removes your will, and not magic. Hammond (2010) reviewed the trials and found hypnosis a useful addition to treatment for anxiety and stress conditions, with effects that depend heavily on the person: about a fifth of people respond strongly to suggestion, about a fifth barely at all, and most sit in between. The relaxation and the rehearsal work for everyone; the suggestion adds more for some. Try each script three times before judging it.

**Why a script for sleep**
Cordi et al. (2014) played a hypnotic suggestion for deep sleep to women before an afternoon nap and measured, in those who were suggestible, about 80% more slow-wave sleep than after a neutral text. One study, a laboratory nap, and a real effect in the people it reached. The script below is the same idea run by you, in bed, at night.

**The script**
Lie on your back, lights out. There is no finish; you are meant to drift off inside it.
Settle. Three slow breaths, and with each exhale sink a little heavier into the mattress.
Release. Move attention slowly from the feet to the head. At each part, think soft, heavy, warm, and let it go: feet, calves, thighs, hips, belly, chest, hands, arms, shoulders, neck, face.
Descend. Picture a slow staircase. Count down from twenty, one number per exhale. With each number, warmer, heavier, further from the day. If the mind wanders, return to the count.
Suggest. Slowly: my body is heavy, my mind is quiet, sleep takes me now. Let the words blur.

**Where it fits**
It sits on top of the Recovery shelf, not instead of it: the fixed wake time, the light and the caffeine cut-off decide whether the body is ready for sleep. The script decides what the mind does with the last ten minutes.$v$, why_it_matters = $v$The last ten minutes before sleep are usually spent replaying the day. A script gives them a job, and the same words every night become a cue the body learns.$v$, try_today = ARRAY[$v$Tonight, in bed, lights out: three breaths, the body release, the count from twenty.$v$, $v$Use the same suggestion line every night so it becomes the cue.$v$, $v$Run it three nights before judging it.$v$], key_takeaways = ARRAY[$v$Self-hypnosis is relaxation, narrowed focus and a chosen suggestion; nothing more.$v$, $v$A fifth of people respond strongly, a fifth barely; the relaxation works for everyone.$v$, $v$Body release, a count down from twenty, one line; you are meant to drift off inside it.$v$], quiz = $v$[{"q": "What is self-hypnosis, in this shelf's terms?", "choices": ["A trance that removes your will", "Relaxation, narrowed attention and a suggestion you choose", "Sleep"], "correct": 1, "explain": "Nothing mystical: the scaffold is settle, count down, suggest, count up, and you are in charge of all of it."}, {"q": "What did Cordi's 2014 study find?", "choices": ["Less sleep", "Nothing", "More slow-wave sleep after a hypnotic suggestion, in suggestible people"], "correct": 2, "explain": "About 80% more deep sleep in a laboratory nap, in the women who responded to suggestion."}, {"q": "How should you judge a script?", "choices": ["After three tries", "Never", "After one try"], "correct": 0, "explain": "The first run is learning the words. The third is the test."}]$v$::jsonb, references_json = $v$[{"author": "Hammond, D. C.", "title": "Hypnosis in the treatment of anxiety- and stress-related disorders (Expert Rev. Neurother.)", "year": 2010}, {"author": "Cordi, M. J., Schlarb, A. A. & Rasch, B.", "title": "Deepening sleep by hypnotic suggestion (Sleep)", "year": 2014}]$v$::jsonb, reflect_prompt = $v$What does your mind do in the last ten minutes before sleep, and who chose that?$v$, integrate_prompt = $v$Three nights of the script. Did you finish the count, and how far did you get?$v$, practice_minutes = 10, lesson_number = 8, display_order = 8, course_role = $v$protocol$v$, read_time_min = 3, updated_at = now() WHERE slug = 'self-hypnosis-sleep';

UPDATE public.vault_articles SET title = $v$Self-Hypnosis: The Pressure Script$v$, subtitle = $v$Calm focus before the big moment, and an honest note on anchors$v$, summary = $v$Ten minutes before the big moment: settle the nerves, rehearse it going well, and pair a physical cue with the state. The cue is a practised habit, not a switch, and the note on that is in the script.$v$, evidence_tier = $v$promising$v$, protocol = $v${"duration": "5 min, 10 to 20 minutes before the moment", "frequency": "Before high-pressure moments; the cue practised daily", "intensity": "Settle, count down, rehearse, pair the cue, count up", "prerequisites": "The real preparation done"}$v$::jsonb, benefits = ARRAY[$v$Nerves read as readiness$v$, $v$A rehearsed state you can recall$v$, $v$A cue with reps behind it$v$], risks = ARRAY[$v$A skill, not a guarantee; it sits on top of preparation$v$, $v$Panic disorder is a clinical matter, not a script$v$, $v$The cue only works with repetition; expect nothing from the first press$v$], body_md = $v$**Before you start**
Read the sleep script's opening if you have not: it explains what these scripts are and what they are not. This one runs 10 to 20 minutes before the moment, somewhere you can close your eyes for five minutes.

**The reframe underneath it**
Brooks (2014) had people say "I am excited" or "I am calm" before public speaking, singing and maths tests. The excited group performed better and felt better than the calm group. The arousal does not have to go; it has to be read as readiness. That finding is the spine of this script.

**The script**
Settle. Three slow breaths, exhale longer than inhale. Tell the nerves: this is energy, and I can use it.
Descend, briefly. Count down from five to one, one number per exhale, into calm focus.
Rehearse. See yourself in the moment doing it well: composed, clear, in command. Hold the image and say, slowly: I am sharp and ready. Pressure makes me focus. I have done the work; now I perform.
Pair a cue. Press thumb and forefinger together while holding the ready feeling. Do this on every rep, so that the press starts to recall the state. Use it as you walk in.
Return. Count up from one to five, eyes open, alert.

**The honest note on the cue**
The thumb-and-finger press is what neuro-linguistic programming calls an anchor, and the claim that a cue installs a state in one go did not survive testing (Witkowski 2010). What does hold is ordinary conditioning: a cue practised many times next to a state becomes a reminder of it, in the way a warm-up song does. So the press is a habit you build over twenty reps, not a switch you flip once. Treat it that way and it earns its place.

**After the moment**
Note what the nerves did and what the first minute felt like. The script improves with a record, and the moments it is for come round again.$v$, why_it_matters = $v$Adrenaline before a big moment is not the enemy; reading it as fear is. Five minutes of rehearsal and one practised cue turn the same arousal into readiness.$v$, try_today = ARRAY[$v$Run the script before the next high-pressure task, ten minutes out.$v$, $v$Practise the thumb-and-finger cue at the end of every rehearsal, so it has reps behind it when you need it.$v$, $v$Say the words 'I am excited' out loud before you walk in, and watch what they do.$v$], key_takeaways = ARRAY[$v$Read the arousal as readiness; the excited group beat the calm group in the trial.$v$, $v$Settle, count down, rehearse, pair the cue, count up.$v$, $v$The cue is conditioning over twenty reps, not a switch; the one-shot claim did not survive testing.$v$], quiz = $v$[{"q": "In Brooks's 2014 experiments, who performed better?", "choices": ["Those who said they were calm", "Those who said they were excited", "No difference"], "correct": 1, "explain": "Reappraising the arousal as excitement beat trying to remove it, across speaking, singing and maths."}, {"q": "What is the honest status of the thumb-and-finger cue?", "choices": ["Useless", "A switch that installs a state in one go", "A cue that becomes a reminder through repeated pairing"], "correct": 2, "explain": "Witkowski 2010 on the one-shot claim; ordinary conditioning on the repeated one. Build it over twenty reps."}]$v$::jsonb, references_json = $v$[{"author": "Brooks, A. W.", "title": "Get excited: reappraising pre-performance anxiety as excitement (J. Exp. Psychol. Gen.)", "year": 2014}, {"author": "Witkowski, T.", "title": "Thirty-five years of research on Neuro-Linguistic Programming. NLP research data base. State of the art or pseudoscientific decoration? (Polish Psychological Bulletin)", "year": 2010}, {"author": "Hammond, D. C.", "title": "Hypnosis in the treatment of anxiety- and stress-related disorders (Expert Rev. Neurother.)", "year": 2010}]$v$::jsonb, reflect_prompt = $v$Before your last big moment, what did the nerves say, and what did you say back?$v$, integrate_prompt = $v$You ran the script and the cue before a real moment. What did the first minute feel like compared with the last time?$v$, practice_minutes = 5, lesson_number = 9, display_order = 9, course_role = $v$protocol$v$, read_time_min = 3, updated_at = now() WHERE slug = 'self-hypnosis-performance-pressure';

UPDATE public.vault_articles SET title = $v$Self-Hypnosis: The Confidence Script$v$, subtitle = $v$Evidence you already have, rehearsed until it is the default$v$, summary = $v$Confidence is not a feeling you wait for. Bandura's word for it is self-efficacy, and its strongest source is your own past performance. This script recalls that evidence in detail and rehearses the state until it is where you start from.$v$, evidence_tier = $v$promising$v$, protocol = $v${"duration": "8 min", "frequency": "Daily for two weeks", "intensity": "Two or three specific wins, relived in detail", "prerequisites": "None"}$v$::jsonb, benefits = ARRAY[$v$A steadier starting state$v$, $v$The critic answered with evidence$v$, $v$Compounds with each day$v$], risks = ARRAY[$v$Supports action; does not replace skill$v$, $v$Low mood or an anxiety disorder needs a clinician, not a script$v$], body_md = $v$**The idea underneath it**
Bandura (1997) spent a career on self-efficacy, the belief that you can do the specific thing in front of you, and found its strongest source is mastery experience: having done it, or something like it, before. Watching others, being told you can, and reading your own arousal all count for less. So a confidence script that runs on affirmations alone runs on the weakest source. This one runs on the strongest: your own record, recalled in detail.

**The script, eight minutes**
Settle. Three slow breaths. Let the shoulders drop.
Descend. Count down from ten to one, one number per exhale, into deep calm.
Evidence. Recall two or three specific moments you handled something well: a time you showed up, finished, did the hard thing. Relive one in detail, what you saw, what you did, what it felt like afterwards. This is proof, not hype, and the detail is what makes it work.
Suggest. Holding that proof, say slowly: I trust myself to handle what comes. I have done hard things, and I can do this. I act with quiet confidence.
Return. Count up from one to five, eyes open, standing a little taller.

**Daily for two weeks**
The script is a rehearsal, and rehearsal needs reps. Two weeks, daily, eight minutes. You are not faking confidence. You are training attention to notice evidence it already has and habitually skips.

**Where it meets the Wisdom shelf**
The Goggins lesson makes the same point from the other side: a kept promise is the smallest unit of self-trust, and the record is what confidence is built from. That lesson is about making the evidence; this one is about reading it.

**What it is not**
A treatment for low mood or an anxiety disorder, and not a substitute for building the skill the moment needs. Confidence without competence is a different problem, with a different fix.$v$, why_it_matters = $v$The inner critic runs on a selective record. The script reads the other half of the record, in detail, every day for two weeks, until the default changes.$v$, try_today = ARRAY[$v$Today, recall one specific win and relive it in detail for two minutes.$v$, $v$Run the full script, eight minutes, daily for two weeks.$v$, $v$Keep a list of the moments you use; the list is the evidence.$v$], key_takeaways = ARRAY[$v$Self-efficacy's strongest source is your own past performance, recalled in detail.$v$, $v$Settle, count down, evidence, suggest, count up; eight minutes.$v$, $v$Two weeks of daily reps; the default shifts with repetition, not intensity.$v$], quiz = $v$[{"q": "What is the strongest source of self-efficacy in Bandura's work?", "choices": ["Your own mastery experience", "Positive affirmations", "Encouragement from others"], "correct": 0, "explain": "Having done it, or something like it, before. The other sources count for less."}, {"q": "Why does the script insist on detail?", "choices": ["Detail is relaxing", "Detail is what makes recalled evidence count as proof", "It fills the time"], "correct": 1, "explain": "A vague memory of a good day is hype. A specific one, relived, is a record."}]$v$::jsonb, references_json = $v$[{"author": "Bandura, A.", "title": "Self-Efficacy: The Exercise of Control", "year": 1997}, {"author": "Hammond, D. C.", "title": "Hypnosis in the treatment of anxiety- and stress-related disorders (Expert Rev. Neurother.)", "year": 2010}]$v$::jsonb, reflect_prompt = $v$Which of your own wins do you never count, and why not?$v$, integrate_prompt = $v$Two weeks of the script. Which moment came up most, and what did it change on the day?$v$, practice_minutes = 8, lesson_number = 10, display_order = 10, course_role = $v$protocol$v$, read_time_min = 2, updated_at = now() WHERE slug = 'self-hypnosis-confidence';

UPDATE public.vault_articles SET title = $v$Self-Hypnosis: The Future-Self Script$v$, subtitle = $v$Rehearse the person you are building, then do one thing they would$v$, summary = $v$A vivid picture of who you are becoming organises today's choices; a vague one does nothing. This script builds the picture in detail and ends with one concrete action, because the picture without the action is the trap the Inner Work shelf warns about.$v$, evidence_tier = $v$promising$v$, protocol = $v${"duration": "10 min", "frequency": "Each morning for a week", "intensity": "A specific future self, one question, one action", "prerequisites": "None"}$v$::jsonb, benefits = ARRAY[$v$A clear picture of who you are becoming$v$, $v$Identity turned into one daily action$v$, $v$Motivation that survives the day$v$], risks = ARRAY[$v$The picture without the action drains drive; never skip the last line$v$, $v$Not a prediction; a rehearsal with homework$v$], body_md = $v$**The idea underneath it**
Markus and Nurius (1986) called them possible selves: the specific versions of yourself you imagine becoming, or fear becoming, and showed that a vivid one shapes present behaviour. Oyserman et al. (2006) tested it with adolescents: those who built detailed possible selves with plans attached did better at school, and the ones without plans did not. The detail and the plan are the active ingredients. This script supplies both, and it ends with a plan of one step.

**The script, ten minutes**
Settle. Three slow breaths, exhale long. Let the day fall away.
Descend. Count down from ten to one, into deep, focused calm.
Meet the future self. Picture yourself one or two years from now, having become the version you are building: disciplined, healthy, calm, strong. See how they stand, move, speak. How do they start the morning? Handle a setback? Treat the people around them? Make it specific enough to recognise on the street.
Suggest and bridge. Slowly: I am becoming this person with every choice. I do what my best self would do. Then ask them one question: what is the single thing I should do today? Take the first answer that comes.
Return. Count up from one to five. Eyes open. Do that one thing.

**Why the action is not optional**
The Inner Work shelf's lesson on manifestation carries the warning: a vivid future enjoyed on its own drains the energy to earn it. The future-self picture is fuel only when it is spent on a step the same day. The last line of the script is the point of the script.

**Daily for a week**
Run it each morning for seven days and log the daily action in the check-in. At the end of the week you will have seven actions and a clearer face for the person you are building; that face is what the Wisdom shelf's Nietzsche and Clear lessons are about.

**What it is not**
A substitute for the work, and not a prediction. It is a rehearsal with a homework line.$v$, why_it_matters = $v$You become the person you repeatedly rehearse being, and only if the rehearsal ends in an action. This script makes both deliberate, in ten minutes, with the action written down.$v$, try_today = ARRAY[$v$Run the script this morning and take the first answer to the question.$v$, $v$Do that one thing today, and log it.$v$, $v$Seven mornings; seven actions.$v$], key_takeaways = ARRAY[$v$A vivid possible self with a plan changes behaviour; a vague wish does not.$v$, $v$Settle, count down, meet the future self, one question, count up, act.$v$, $v$The last line is the point: one concrete action, today.$v$], quiz = $v$[{"q": "What made possible selves work in Oyserman's 2006 study?", "choices": ["Reading about role models", "Optimism alone", "Detail plus a plan attached"], "correct": 2, "explain": "The detailed selves with plans moved behaviour; the ones without plans did not."}, {"q": "Why must the script end in an action?", "choices": ["A vivid future enjoyed on its own drains the energy to earn it", "To fill ten minutes", "Tradition"], "correct": 0, "explain": "Kappes and Oettingen's finding on positive fantasy. The picture is fuel only when it is spent the same day."}]$v$::jsonb, references_json = $v$[{"author": "Markus, H. & Nurius, P.", "title": "Possible selves (Am. Psychol.)", "year": 1986}, {"author": "Oyserman, D., Bybee, D. & Terry, K.", "title": "Possible selves and academic outcomes: how and when possible selves impel action (J. Pers. Soc. Psychol.)", "year": 2006}, {"author": "Kappes, H. B. & Oettingen, G.", "title": "Positive fantasies about idealized futures sap energy (J. Exp. Soc. Psychol.)", "year": 2011}]$v$::jsonb, reflect_prompt = $v$Describe the person you are building in one sentence a stranger could recognise them by.$v$, integrate_prompt = $v$Seven mornings, seven actions. Which one would the future self have chosen, and which did you skip?$v$, practice_minutes = 10, lesson_number = 11, display_order = 11, course_role = $v$protocol$v$, read_time_min = 3, updated_at = now() WHERE slug = 'self-hypnosis-best-self';
